<h1>Help Change Labor Laws for Parents of Children with Special Needs</h1>

<p>Millions of parents across the U.S. are silently struggling—not from a lack of motivation, but from a lack of support. Parents of children with special needs face unique challenges that make traditional work schedules nearly impossible.</p>
<p>We need your voice to push Congress to update labor laws so parents can work <strong>and</strong> care for their children without impossible sacrifices.</p>

<div class="video-container">
  <iframe src="https://www.tiktok.com/embed/v2/7496127666588749087" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</div>

<p><strong>Ready-to-use letter to your local Congressman:</strong></p>

<textarea id="letterText" readonly>
Dear [Representative's Name],

I am a parent of a child with special needs and I am writing to ask you to support critical labor law reforms to help families like mine.

Millions of parents are struggling not because we lack motivation, but because we lack support. Traditional work schedules make it nearly impossible for us to balance employment and caregiving.

We urge Congress and the U.S. Department of Labor to:

- Require employers to offer flexible scheduling or remote work options for parents of children with documented special needs, where job duties allow.
- Expand protections under the Family and Medical Leave Act (FMLA) to better cover part-time workers and those in smaller companies.
- Offer tax incentives or subsidies to employers who accommodate special needs parents.
- Promote inclusive hiring practices that do not penalize applicants for their caregiving responsibilities.

Every parent deserves the dignity of earning a living without sacrificing their child’s well-being. Please support compassionate labor reform to create a future where no parent must choose between financial stability and being present for their child.

Thank you for your time and consideration.

Sincerely,
[Your Name]
[Your Address]
[Your Contact Information]
</textarea>

<button onclick="copyLetter()">Copy Letter to Clipboard</button>

